  "query_preview": "..\nSunday through Monday night: A cold upper-level low centered over \nthe Pacific Northwest, combined with surface high pressure building \neast of the northern Rockies, will strengthen the regional northeast \npressure gradient. The Cranbrook, BC to Pasco, WA (YXC-PSC) gradient \nis forecast to tighten from ~7 mb Saturday evening to ~12 mb early \nSunday morning. This will generate gusty northeast winds through the \nPurcell Trench into the Spokane area and Columbia Basin through \nSunday afternoon. Coupled with a dry air mass (precipitable water \nvalues 60-80% of normal; dew points in the mid 20s to low 30s), \nthese winds will increase the risk for blowing dust and localized \nvisibility reductions across the central and western Columbia Basin, \nincluding Moses Lake, Coulee City, Wilbur, and Ritzville on Sunday.\n\nAdditionally, these winds will draw cold air from the northeast into \nthe Inland Northwest, with snow levels falling to between 2500 to \n3000 feet across northeast Washington and north Idaho Sunday \nmorning. Precipitation wrapping around the upper-level low as it \ndrifts southward Sunday will fall as snow primarily across the \nmountains of the central Idaho Panhandle, though isolated flakes \ncould fall to higher elevations across the Palouse early Sunday \nmorning.\n\nSunday will be the coldest day of this week with highs mostly in the \nupper 40s to upper 50s. Low temperatures will fall into the mid to \nupper 20s across the northern Valleys Sunday and Monday nights. \nWinds may be strong enough across the Columbia Basin and Spokane \narea Sunday night to keep temperatures above freezing with a 30-60% \nchance for temperatures below 32F. As the low moves south of \nthe area Monday, building upper-level ridging will bring lighter\nwinds and clearing skies. With dry air remaining in place, the \nprobability of sub-freezing temperatures across the Spokane area\nand Columbia Basin increases to 60-80% Monday night. The \nPalouse has better chances for freezing temperatures both nights\nwith a 70% chance Sunday night and a 100% chance Monday night. \n\nTuesday through Saturday: Temperatures begin to trend warmer for the \nrest of the week as an upper-level ridge builds over the Pacific \nNorthwest.  A weak front on Wednesday flattens the ridge temporarily \nand brings an increase in clouds, a 15% chance for showers across \nthe north Cascades and breezy mountain winds. The ridge strengthens \nThursday through Saturday with afternoon temperatures warming into \nthe mid 60s to low 70s by Friday. These temperatures are 8-13 \ndegrees above normal for this time of year. Overnight lows will \nremain chilly though, with values in the upper 20s to low 30s across \nthe northern values and in the low to mid 30s elsewhere. Ensembles \nbegin to show the next weather system moving into the Pacific \nNorthwest between late Friday night and Saturday, though there \nremains differences on the timing and strength. The main message at \nthis point is that this will be the next period to monitor for \nbreezy winds and mountain showers. /vmt",
